\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Scalar fields on group orbits}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{A sign attached to each vertex can favor agreement across an edge.
Flipping every sign preserves each agreement, even in a configuration
with many disagreements. Which features of the scalar field survive
averaging over configurations, and what changes when an external field
favors one sign?}

\p{The [orbit gauge–Higgs weight](fdip-010A) compares neighboring
values by transporting one along the intervening link. This allows the two endpoints
to change frames independently.}

\p{Choosing [scalar frames](fdip-010X) relates joint gauge orbits
to reduced links. A [path pairing](fdip-011B) measures their
transport, and an [onsite Yukawa operator](fdip-011M) couples
the scalar to the fibre on each based cell.}

\p{When the scalar occupies several orbits, \ref{fdip-0123}
identifies the invariant functions with functions of the reduced links
modulo the product of the residual groups, and the number of scalar
values in each orbit enters as a counting factor (\ref{fdip-0124}). On
one edge the [operator](fdip-012C) preserves the functions constant on
double cosets, their number is a sum of squares of invariant dimensions
(\ref{fdip-013G}), and the declared kernel values with the double-coset
sizes determine the characteristic polynomial (\ref{fdip-013H}). When a
real symmetric matrix has the orthonormal eigenbasis recorded in
\ref{fdip-011Y}, its correlations are finite sums over those
eigenvectors (\ref{fdip-012W}). A rank-one update gives the determinant
and a diagonal bound (\ref{fdip-013I}), and environment constants
rescale each sector sum and leave the one-edge matrix unchanged
(\ref{fdip-013A}).}

